		<div class="col-sm-7 padding-20-top content-column"> <!-- InstanceBeginEditable name="6col_content" -->
		
			
			
			<p>The Puget Sound Partnership organizes  a Community Advocacy Council (CAC) made up of leaders from around the Puget  Sound, working to make their communities healthier, stronger, and safer for  all. One focus of the CAC is to gather resources that council members identify  as most important to the communities that they serve. The group&rsquo;s goal is to  share those resources with any community that may find them useful. On this  page, you can find a collection of resources gathered by the CAC. Resources are  organized by topic area. </p>
        <p>If you have a resource to suggest  that we add to this list, or if you have questions about any of these  resources, please contact us at environmental.justice@psp.wa.gov.</p>
        <p><strong>Note</strong>: As the Community Advocacy Council is  piloting this effort, they have focused on adding resources related to water  quality. The council hopes to add resources related to other environmental  justice topics in the future.<br>
        </p>
        <h2>WATER QUALITY</h2>
            <h3>Groups focusing on climate justice for youth related to water quality and  beyond:            </h3>
            <h4>YMCA Earth Service Corps</h4>
        <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong><em>:</em> <a href="https://www.seattleymca.org/programs/youthprograms/earthservicecorps/about-us">About  Us | YMCA of Greater Seattle</a></li>
              <li><strong>Description</strong><em>:</em> This program on the  YMCA works with middle and high-school-aged youth in the greater Seattle area  to provide education, hands-on experience, and leadership training. Climate  justice and water quality is one of many topics that they focus on.</li>
              <li><strong>Contact</strong><em>: </em>Chelsey Wheeler,  Director, <a href="mailto:cwheeler@seattleymca.org">cwheeler@seattleymca.org</a></li>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Notes</strong><em>: </em>The council may be able to introduce  you to a contact here.</li>
              </ul>
            </ul>
            <h4>Dirt Corps</h4>
        <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong><em>:</em> <a href="https://www.thedirtcorps.com/">The Dirt Corps - Dirt Corps</a></li>
              <li><strong>Description</strong><em>:</em> This organization  operates in South Seattle and is focused on providing paid on-the-job training  focused on topics including Green Infrastructure, Ecological Restoration and  Urban Forestry. They also host volunteer events</li>
              <li><strong>Contact</strong><em>: </em>Andrew Schiffer,  Principal &amp; Director of Community Partnerships, <a href="mailto:andrew@thedirtcorps.com">andrew@thedirtcorps.com</a></li>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Notes</strong><em>: </em>The council may be able to introduce  you to a contact here.<br>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </ul>
            <h4>King County Parks Youth Conservation Corps</h4>
        <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong><em>:</em> <a href="https://kcpyouthcorps.org/">King County Parks Youth Conservation Corps</a></li>
              <li><strong>Description</strong><em>:</em> They offer  a paid summer internship program that teaches youth about conservation. Their  projects seem to lean towards a restoration focus.</li>
          <li><strong>Contact</strong><em>: </em>Sahar Arbab, Youth  Conservation Corps Leader, <a href="mailto:ycc@kingcounty.gov">ycc@kingcounty.gov</a></li>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Notes</strong><em>: </em>The council may be able to introduce  you to a contact here.            </li>
          </ul>
            </ul>
            <h4>Pierce County &lsquo;s Environmental Education Team            </h4>
            <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong><em>: </em><a href="https://www.piercecountywa.gov/1555/Environmental-Education">Environmental  Education | Pierce County, WA - Official Website</a></li>
              <li><strong>Description</strong><em>: </em>This team provides  free/low-cost classes around environmental topics in schools. This group  supports youth programming and networking opportunities focused on climate  justice and other topics. They also have specific initiative work focused on  topics like food waste, rain gardens, and more. Their <a href="https://www.piercecountywa.gov/1586/Earth-Matters-Newsletter">Earth  Matters Newsletter</a> is also a great resource to learn new tools and tips for  the waste reduction and recycling.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Storming the Sound Conference</h4>
            <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong>: <a href="https://www.pacname.org/regional-chapters/washington/storming-the-sound/">Storming  the Sound - Northwest Aquatic and Marine Educators</a></li>
              <li><strong>Description</strong>: &ldquo;Storming  the Sound is a conference for environmental educators in the north Puget Sound  region, including the counties of Whatcom, Skagit, Snohomish, Island, Jefferson  and San Juan. Every January teachers, environmental organizations and students  gather to share their interest and expertise in environmental education&rdquo;  (source: website home page). This event tackles climate justice, Puget Sound  issues, and more.            </li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Education Partnerships subject matter expert group for the Action Agenda</h4>
        <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Description</strong><em>: </em>A semi-official group  that is charged with implementing the Education Partnerships strategy for the <a href="https://www.psp.wa.gov/2022AAupdate.php">Action Agenda</a>.</li>
              <li><strong>Contact</strong><em>: </em>Contact Puget Sound  Partnership Staff (environmental.justice@psp.wa.gov) for an introduction to the  group.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Groups working to measure stormwater temperature (for the purpose of  combatting urban heat islands):            </h3>
            <h4>Puget Small Streams Study</h4>
        <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong>: <a href="https://ecology.wa.gov/regulations-permits/reporting-requirements/stormwater-monitoring/stormwater-action-monitoring/sam-status-and-trends/puget-small-streams">Puget  Small Streams Study</a></li>
              <li><strong>Description</strong>: Puget Small Streams Study is  a part of the program under &ldquo;Stormwater Action Monitoring (SAM)&rdquo; led by the  collaboration between the Department of Ecology and municipal stormwater  permittees. SAM is a program that monitors the status of stormwater in Western  Washington. Puget Small Streams Study is interested in monitoring stormwater in  urban conditions and how it affects small streams in Puget Sound.  </li>
              <li><strong>Puget Small Streams Study data</strong>: <a href="https://www.sciencebase.gov/catalog/item/67d18f12d34ecfe34cc88469">Puget  Small Streams Status and Trends Monitoring Project - ScienceBase-Catalog</a></li>
            </ul>
        <h4>Stormwater Characterization</h4>
        <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><strong>Website</strong>: <a href="https://ecology.wa.gov/regulations-permits/reporting-requirements/stormwater-monitoring/stormwater-action-monitoring/sam-effectiveness-studies/stormwater-characterization">Stormwater  characterization - Washington State Department of Ecology</a></li>
          <li><strong>Description</strong>: The City of Tacoma will  study 16 different sites to determine their differences according to their land  uses (industrial, commercial, residential), and to discover stormwater trends. They  also collect and sample stormwater and stormwater sediment.</li>
          </ul>
            <h2>Key resources in <a href="https://www.pugetsoundinfo.wa.gov/#goals">Puget  Sound Info</a></h2>
            <ul class="bullet-size-fix">
              <li><a href="https://vitalsigns.pugetsoundinfo.wa.gov/VitalSignGoal/Detail/6">Healthy  Water Quality Section</a>: provides an overview of water quality in Puget  Sound, why it&rsquo;s an important topic, how it has changed over the years, and what  big issues of concern are.</li>
              <ul>
                <li>At the bottom of the page, expand any of the  vital signs (Freshwater, Marine Water, or Toxics in Aquatic Life) to find more  specific information, data, and reports about how the Puget Sound is doing on  each vital sign indicator (for example, by selecting the &ldquo;contaminants in adult  salmon&rdquo; indicator, you can find data about contaminants in adult salmon).</li>
                <li>If you select a vital sign header, such as  &ldquo;Freshwater&rdquo;, you can find background documents on the vital sign, and  information about related strategies that this work interconnects with.</li>
              </ul>
              <li><a href="https://nepatlas.pugetsoundinfo.wa.gov/Activity/Index">National Estuary  Program activities list</a>: use the search bar to look up keywords and find  events/opportunities/activities that you may want to get involved in.</li>
              <li><a href="https://www.pugetsoundinfo.wa.gov/OngoingProgram/Programs">Ongoing  Programs Portal program list</a>: use the search bar to look up keywords, such  as &ldquo;salmon&rdquo;, to find programs and work that we are connected to and that are  related to topics you are interested in.            </li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Resources for home owners, small businesses, residents, etc.:</h2>
            <h3>SEATTLE</h3>
